is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: dns_server
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: DNS service (BIND/named) on dns_server with incorrect recursion policy and missing upstream resolution path. Mechanism: Clients resolve internal names (web0.local, etc.) quickly because the server is authoritative for zone “local,” but any lookup outside that zone is refused. Many apps/pages try to fetch external resources (CDNs, telemetry, favicons), so DNS failures manifest as pages “spinning” until timeouts/caches change, then seeming to recover. Localization: On dns_server, named is running and listening (netstat shows 10.200.0.2:53). Host resolvers point only to 10.200.0.2 (/etc/resolv.conf). A query from host_1_1_1_1 returns REFUSED for google.com (dig/nslookup). The BIND config explicitly disables recursion: /etc/bind/named.conf contains “recursion no;” and only defines “zone ‘local’ … file ‘/etc/bind/db.local’;” (where web0–web3 A records live). There is no include of default root hints or forwarders, so even if recursion were enabled, upstream resolution would be unavailable. Root cause: BIND was configured as authoritative-only (recursion disabled) without any forwarders or root hints, causing all non-local lookups to be refused. Corrective fix: On dns_server, enable recursion for internal clients and provide an upstream path. For example in named.conf: set “recursion yes;” and either include “named.conf.default-zones” (root hints) or configure forwarders (e.g., to approved external resolvers), plus “allow-recursion { 10.0.0.0/8; 127.0.0.1; };”. Then reload named. After this, internal names will remain instant and external dependencies will resolve consistently. No service restart was needed for performance; this is a configuration issue. 
=== END ===